<!--/Post: 2412-->
<!--Post: 2418--><!--Time: 1103622994-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2418"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 21, 2004 - 1:56 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If I&#39;m following correctly, then my query of the QRZ database showed the person in question&#39;s initials to be THB. His profession and the ham thing match up, but I could find no evidence of him or his e-mail address showing up on any railfan sites. I&#39;m not outright refuting your suspicions. They are still understandable. Let&#39;s just, when checking out someone on the internet, be sure to not try shoehorn facts inside the mold of a theory. It&#39;s not fair to the person in question and a little dangerous on the legal end of things. <BR> <BR>Just a note of caution more than one of argument, though. <BR> <BR>Steve<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2418-->
<!--Post: 2424--><!--Time: 1103644359-->
